{"id": "sevt_01AS3sB5SH9atxMYHcahLKd1", "input": {"evidence": "app/routes/orders.py:10 registers the public route GET /orders. No authentication decorator or check is visible. app/routes/orders.py:12 reads `customer` directly from request.args, so an attacker controls it. app/routes/orders.py:16 builds the SQL by string concatenation: \"SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer = '\" + customer + \"'\". app/routes/orders.py:17 runs it with conn.execute(sql) and no bound parameters. app/routes/orders.py:18 returns every row to the caller. The comment at app/routes/orders.py:14-15 says the input is validated upstream, but the repository contains no such validation. The only other file, config.py:3, just sets DB_PATH. The repository has no before_request hooks, validators or sanitizers. Example payload: ?customer=' OR '1'='1 returns every customer's orders. UNION-based payloads can read other tables in the SQLite database. sqlite3's execute() rejects stacked statements, so the main impact is data disclosure, not arbitrary writes.", "finding_id": "F-108", "recommended_fix": "Use a parameterized query: conn.execute(\"SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer = ?\", (customer,)).fetchall(). Also add authentication and authorization so users can only list their own orders. Close the connection, for example with a context manager. Remove the misleading \"security-reviewed\" comment at lines 14-15, because the input is not validated anywhere.", "severity": "high", "title": "SQL injection in GET /orders via the 'customer' query parameter", "verdict": "exploitable"}, "name": "create_ticket_draft", "processed_at": "2026-10-26T20:56:25.216845Z", "type": "agent.custom_tool_use"}
